<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pays de Retz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pays de Retz is a historic district south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> estuary in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a> and the Atlantic coast, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Loire-Atlantique. It was ceded to the Breton king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> in 851 and remained part of Brittany thereafter. Its later lords included Gilles de Rais, the 15th-century companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pays-de-retz.jpg" alt="Pays de Retz" width="222" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orror · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pays_de_Retz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
